<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">French Florida</h1><p class="st-meta">1562 CE – 1565 CE · capital Fort Caroline</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Huguenot colony on the St Johns River whose destruction by Spain in 1565 secured Florida for the Spanish crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Backed by Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspard-de-coligny">Gaspard de Coligny</a>, the Protestant captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ribault">Jean Ribault</a> sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1562 and left a small garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlesfort">Charlesfort</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parris-island">Parris Island</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, which was soon abandoned. In 1564 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-goulaine-de-laudonniere">René Goulaine de Laudonnière</a> brought a second expedition and built Fort Caroline near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St Johns River</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was a fortified outpost of a few hundred soldiers, artisans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> settlers rather than a governed territory. It depended on food from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturiwa">Saturiwa</a> and his neighbours, and hunger and mutiny troubled it. The artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-le-moyne-de-morgues">Jacques Le Moyne de Morgues</a> drew the Timucua, leaving some of the earliest European images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1565 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a>, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-augustine-florida">St Augustine</a>, captured Fort Caroline and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ribault">Ribault</a> and his shipwrecked men at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas-inlet">Matanzas Inlet</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominique-de-gourgues">Dominique de Gourgues</a> avenged them in 1568, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> did not return, leaving <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a> in control.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-florida.jpg" alt="French Florida" width="320" height="465"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Nicolas Bellin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_Florida_1562.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1562 CE – 1565 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Caroline</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jean Ribault, René Goulaine de Laudonnière</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fort Caroline, La Caroline</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1564&amp;focus=french-florida" aria-label="French Florida on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2566 1069 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Florida lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2886 1325L5054 728M2886 1325L2886 1342"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M2886 1342h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2886 1325h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2886 1325h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fort Caroline. <a href="/atlas?year=1564&amp;focus=french-florida">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

